I recently built a small Linux kernel module: slab_uaf_detector.c — a learning-driven experiment to understand how the kernel handles memory safety at the slab allocator level.

The goal was not to “reinvent security tools”, but to deeply observe how Use-After-Free (UAF), double free patterns, canary corruption, and RCU-based lifetime issues manifest inside kernel space.

🔧 What I implemented

The module creates a controlled object lifecycle using:
- kmem_cache backed allocations (SLAB)
- custom object metadata tracking (id, name, freed flag)
- canary guards (0xDEADBEEF) for memory integrity checks
- RCU-based deferred reclamation
- /proc/slab_uaf_stats interface for runtime visibility
- tracepoints via trace_printk for execution flow analysis

It intentionally simulates:
- Use-after-free detection
- Double free detection
- Canary corruption detection
- Stale access visibility under RCU delay

🧠 Key insights from building it

1. “freed != immediately invalid” in kernel reality  
Kernel objects often survive logically after free due to RCU and slab reuse delays.

2. Memory corruption is observable before it becomes fatal  
SLAB_POISON and red zones make silent corruption visible, not just destructive.

3. Tracing is as important as detection logic  
trace_pipe showed lifecycle transitions far more clearly than printk alone.

4. Modpost / build system strictness is part of kernel engineering  
Even module metadata issues (like MODULE_LICENSE misinterpretation) teach how tightly the kernel enforces correctness.

🔬 Broader engineering alignment

This experiment sits at the intersection of:
- Linux kernel memory management (SLAB allocator internals)
- Reliability engineering (fault detection before crash)
- Security instrumentation (UAF detection patterns)
- Systems observability (tracepoints + /proc introspection)

It also reinforces a core kernel principle:

“You don’t debug memory in the kernel — you instrument it.”

🚀 Next direction

The next iteration of this work would evolve toward:
- kprobe/ftrace-based lifecycle tracking
- KASAN-aligned detection patterns
- real-time memory anomaly visualization
- OT/embedded Linux safety monitoring concepts

This is still early-stage exploration, but it’s a meaningful step toward understanding how low-level memory safety tooling is actually designed inside production kernels.

I recently spent time building a Linux kernel-space experimental module: slab_uaf_detector.c — not as a “finished tool”, but as a systems learning probe into how memory safety actually behaves inside the Linux kernel.

The motivation was simple: most discussions around Use-After-Free (UAF), double-free, and memory corruption stay at the conceptual or userspace sanitizer level (ASan/KASAN). I wanted to observe what happens when you deliberately instrument the kernel slab allocator path itself.

---

🔧 What this module actually does

At its core, the module builds a controlled object lifecycle on top of kmem_cache:

• SLAB-backed object allocation via kmem_cache_alloc  
• Explicit lifecycle state tracking (active → freed → accessed)  
• Canary guards (0xDEADBEEF) to detect silent corruption  
• RCU-based deferred reclamation model  
• Object registry using list_head for live tracking  
• /proc interface (/proc/slab_uaf_stats) for runtime introspection  
• trace_printk hooks feeding into trace_pipe for live kernel tracing  

On top of that, it intentionally simulates unsafe scenarios:
- Use-after-free access detection
- Double-free detection
- Post-free access visibility under delayed reclamation
- Canary corruption detection under slab poisoning scenarios

---

🧠 Key kernel-level insights discovered

1. Memory “free” is not an immediate state change  
In kernel space, freeing an object does not mean it is instantly invalid. With SLAB reuse and RCU grace periods, objects can remain logically reachable while physically reallocated or poisoned.

This breaks the intuitive userspace assumption of:
free() == invalid memory

Kernel reality is closer to:
free() == lifecycle transition with delayed invalidation

---

2. SLAB poisoning turns silent failures into observable artifacts  
Enabling SLAB_POISON and red zones transforms memory corruption from undefined behavior into detectable state transitions.

Instead of silent crashes, we can observe:
- overwritten canaries
- corrupted metadata
- delayed fault manifestation

This is foundational for kernel debugging strategy.

---

3. Instrumentation matters more than logic  
The actual detection logic (freed flag checks, canary verification) is secondary compared to observability mechanisms:

- trace_pipe gives temporal execution ordering
- printk gives coarse-grained state
- /proc gives structural state snapshot

Together they form a multi-layer introspection model.

---

4. Kernel build system is part of the learning curve  
Even before runtime behavior, modpost validation, MODULE_LICENSE enforcement, and Kbuild dependency rules enforce strict correctness.

This is not just compilation — it is static validation of kernel trust metadata.

---

🔬 How this fits into broader kernel engineering

This experiment sits at the intersection of:

• Linux kernel memory management internals (SLAB allocator behavior)  
• Reliability engineering (fault detection before system failure)  
• Security instrumentation (UAF and memory corruption visibility)  
• Observability engineering (tracepoints + procfs introspection)  
• Systems-level debugging design patterns  

More importantly, it reflects a core principle of kernel development:

“You don’t observe memory safety in the kernel by trusting state — you observe it by instrumenting transitions.”

